2025-04-18 09:14:34,868 - security_agent - INFO - Received response from Heroku AI: {'response': '# Privilege Escalation Investigation Analysis\n\n## 1. Security Implications\n\nPrivilege escalation attacks allow adversaries to gain higher-level permissions than initially granted, creating significant security risks:\n\n• **Expanded Attack Surface**: Elevated privileges enable access to sensitive systems, data, and configuration settings previously protected\n• **Persistence Establishment**: Attackers can create backdoors, modify startup processes, or install rootkits that survive reboots\n• **Defense Evasion**: Higher privileges allow attackers to disable security tools, clear logs, and hide malicious activities\n• **Lateral Movement**: Elevated access facilitates movement across network segments and systems\n• **Data Exfiltration**: Privileged access enables extraction of sensitive information that may be protected from standard users\n\n## 2. Specific Indicators to Monitor\n\n### New Service Creation\n• Unusual services created outside of change management windows\n• Services with suspicious names, misspellings, or randomized characters\n• Services running from non-standard locations (e.g., temp directories, user profiles)\n• Services using unexpected executable paths or command line parameters\n• Services running under SYSTEM privileges without legitimate business need\n\n### Scheduled Task Modification\n• New tasks created by non-administrative users\n• Tasks scheduled during off-hours or at unusual intervals\n• Tasks referencing scripts/executables in temporary or user directories\n• Tasks with obfuscated command arguments or encoded PowerShell commands\n• Modifications to existing legitimate scheduled tasks\n\n### Unusual Process Elevation\n• Unexpected use of UAC bypass techniques\n• Applications requesting elevation without historical precedent\n• Processes spawning child processes with higher privileges\n• Use of known vulnerable applications that can be exploited for privilege escalation\n• Unexpected usage of tools like PsExec, RunAs, or similar privilege management utilities\n\n### Admin Group Changes\n• Additions to privileged groups outside of change control processes\n• Temporary elevation that isn\'t reverted within expected timeframes\n• Creation of new administrative accounts during irregular hours\n• Modifications to built-in groups like "Administrators," "Domain Admins," or "Enterprise Admins"\n• Changes to security group nesting that indirectly grant elevated permissions\n\n## 3. Investigation Steps\n\n1. **Initial Triage**\n   • Isolate affected systems if active compromise is suspected\n   • Capture volatile data and memory dumps before system shutdown\n   • Establish a timeline of suspected activities\n   • Document all investigation steps for potential legal proceedings\n\n2. **Log Analysis**\n   • Review Windows Event Logs (Security, System, Application)\n   • Examine PowerShell logs (Event ID 4104) for suspicious script execution\n   • Analyze authentication logs for unexpected privilege changes (Event IDs 4728, 4732, 4756)\n   • Review service creation and modification events (Event IDs 7045, 4697)\n   • Check for scheduled task creation and modification (Event ID 4698, 4702)\n\n3. **System Examination**\n   • Analyze running processes and their parent-child relationships\n   • Review installed services and their configurations\n   • Examine scheduled tasks and their execution history\n   • Audit local and domain group memberships\n   • Check for unauthorized or suspicious binaries in startup locations\n\n4. **Network Analysis**\n   • Review network connections from suspected compromised hosts\n   • Look for unusual outbound connections following privilege changes\n   • Analyze lateral movement attempts following escalation\n   • Check for data exfiltration patterns post-compromise\n\n5. **Forensic Analysis**\n   • Perform disk forensics to identify file system changes\n   • Analyze registry for persistence mechanisms and modifications\n   • Review user profile and application data for signs of compromise\n   • Examine artifacts from common privilege escalation techniques\n\n## 4. Mitigation Strategies\n\n• **Immediate Containment**\n  - Revoke elevated privileges granted to compromised accounts\n  - Remove unauthorized services and scheduled tasks\n  - Isolate affected systems from the network\n  - Reset credentials for affected accounts\n\n• **Short-term Remediation**\n  - Patch vulnerable software and operating systems\n  - Remove unnecessary administrative privileges\n  - Implement application allowlisting\n  - Enable Protected Process Light for critical services\n  - Deploy endpoint detection and response (EDR) solutions\n\n• **Long-term Prevention**\n  - Implement comprehensive privilege access management\n  - Establish robust change management procedures\n  - Deploy file integrity monitoring for critical system files\n  - Conduct regular privilege audits and cleanup\n  -'}
