2025-04-18 09:17:30,171 - security_agent - INFO - Received response from Heroku AI: {'response': "# Critical Security Alerts for Junior Analysts\n\n## 1. Security Implications Analysis\n\nEach critical alert category represents a potential security breach with significant implications:\n\n* **Failed Authentication Attempts**: May indicate password spraying, credential stuffing, or brute force attacks targeting your organization's accounts. Successful breaches can lead to unauthorized access, data exfiltration, and lateral movement.\n\n* **Malware Detections**: Signals that malicious code has potentially bypassed perimeter defenses. Malware can establish persistence, exfiltrate data, encrypt files for ransom, or create backdoors for future access.\n\n* **Suspicious PowerShell/Command Line Activity**: Often indicates living-off-the-land techniques where attackers use legitimate system tools to execute malicious commands, evading traditional security controls and operating with elevated privileges.\n\n* **Unusual Service Creations**: May represent persistence mechanisms where attackers install services to maintain access across system reboots or to execute privileged code without user interaction.\n\n## 2. Specific Indicators to Monitor\n\n### Failed Authentication Attempts\n* Multiple failures from the same source IP address\n* Authentication attempts outside normal business hours\n* Geographic anomalies (logins from unusual countries)\n* Failed attempts across multiple accounts in short timeframes\n* Successful authentication following multiple failures (potential breach)\n* Authentication attempts for disabled or dormant accounts\n\n### Malware Detections\n* Alerts from endpoint protection platforms\n* Unusual file hashes or digital signatures\n* Files with multiple extensions (e.g., invoice.pdf.exe)\n* Unexpected outbound connections to known malicious domains/IPs\n* Abnormal system resource usage\n* Modified system files or unexpected registry changes\n\n### Suspicious PowerShell/Command Line Activity\n* Obfuscated or encoded PowerShell commands\n* PowerShell execution with bypass flags (-ExecutionPolicy Bypass)\n* Base64 encoded commands\n* PowerShell downloading content from external sources\n* Command line tools accessing unusual network destinations\n* Use of uncommon command line parameters or switches\n\n### Unusual Service Creations\n* Services created outside change management windows\n* Services with random or misleading names\n* Services running from non-standard locations\n* Services with unusual command line parameters\n* Services created by unexpected user accounts\n* Services with descriptions that don't match their names\n\n## 3. Investigation Steps\n\n### For Failed Authentication Attempts\n1. Identify affected username(s) and source IP address(es)\n2. Check if the account was eventually compromised (successful login after failures)\n3. Review user activity logs for any post-authentication anomalies\n4. Correlate with other security events from the same source/destination\n5. Determine if the account owner was traveling or working remotely\n6. Check for similar patterns across other accounts\n\n### For Malware Detections\n1. Isolate affected systems from the network\n2. Preserve forensic evidence before remediation\n3. Identify malware type, capabilities, and infection vector\n4. Determine scope of infection (single system vs. multiple systems)\n5. Check for data exfiltration indicators\n6. Review system logs for initial access indicators\n\n### For Suspicious PowerShell/Command Line Activity\n1. Capture the full command line for analysis\n2. Decode any obfuscated commands\n3. Determine the user context of execution\n4. Identify created files, modified registry keys, or network connections\n5. Review process lineage (parent-child relationships)\n6. Check for persistence mechanisms established\n\n### For Unusual Service Creations\n1. Document service properties (name, path, startup parameters, account)\n2. Verify if service creation aligns with change management records\n3. Analyze the executable or script the service is configured to run\n4. Check service account privileges\n5. Review surrounding events in system and security logs\n6. Determine if other systems have similar service creations\n\n## 4. Potential Mitigation Strategies\n\n### For Failed Authentication Attempts\n* Implement account lockout policies after multiple failures\n* Enable multi-factor authentication (MFA)\n* Implement IP-based access restrictions where feasible\n* Alert on or block authentication attempts from high-risk countries\n* Implement CAPTCHA for external-facing authentication portals\n* Consider using adaptive authentication based on risk scores\n\n### For Malware Detections\n* Update antivirus/EDR signatures and engines\n* Patch vulnerable software and operating systems\n* Restrict execution permissions in high-risk folders\n* Implement application whitel"}
