2025-04-18 10:42:49,306 - security_agent - INFO - Received response from Heroku AI: {'response': '# Critical Security Alerts: Analysis and Response Guide\n\n## 1. Security Implications Analysis\n\nCritical security alerts for junior analysts encompass high-risk activities that often indicate active threats:\n\n* **Failed Authentication Attempts**: May indicate password guessing, brute force attacks, or credential stuffing attempts. Persistent failures could signal an attacker attempting to gain unauthorized system access.\n\n* **Malware Detections**: Represent successful delivery of malicious code to endpoints, potentially leading to data exfiltration, ransomware deployment, or establishment of command-and-control channels.\n\n* **Suspicious PowerShell/Command Line Activity**: Often indicates "living off the land" techniques where attackers use legitimate system tools to execute malicious activities while evading traditional security controls.\n\n* **Unusual Service Creations**: May signal persistence mechanisms being established by attackers to maintain access across system reboots or enable privilege escalation.\n\nThese alerts typically represent active exploitation attempts or successful compromise requiring immediate investigation and response.\n\n## 2. Specific Indicators to Monitor\n\n### Failed Authentication Attempts\n* Multiple failures from the same source IP address\n* Failed attempts across numerous accounts in short timeframes\n* Geographic anomalies (logins attempted from unusual locations)\n* Authentication attempts outside normal business hours\n* Failed attempts followed by successful logins (indicating successful credential cracking)\n\n### Malware Detections\n* File hashes matching known malware signatures\n* Unexpected outbound network connections from infected hosts\n* DNS requests to known malicious domains\n* Unusual registry modifications\n* Creation of scheduled tasks or auto-start mechanisms\n\n### Suspicious PowerShell/Command Line Activity\n* Obfuscated or encoded commands (Base64 encoding)\n* PowerShell execution policy bypasses\n* Commands that download and execute content from the internet\n* Memory manipulation commands or reflective loading techniques\n* Unusual parent-child process relationships (e.g., Office applications spawning PowerShell)\n\n### Unusual Service Creations\n* Services with random names or masquerading as legitimate services\n* Services created with high privileges (SYSTEM)\n* Services pointing to executables in temporary or user directories\n* Services created outside of change management windows\n* Binary replacements of legitimate service executables\n\n## 3. Recommended Investigation Steps\n\n### For Failed Authentication Attempts:\n1. Identify affected accounts and the source of attempts\n2. Determine if any attempts were successful\n3. Review account activity for signs of compromise\n4. Check for related suspicious activities across affected systems\n5. Correlate with other security events in the same timeframe\n\n### For Malware Detections:\n1. Isolate affected systems from the network\n2. Capture memory dumps and disk images before remediation if possible\n3. Identify malware type and associated IOCs (Indicators of Compromise)\n4. Determine infection vector and initial access point\n5. Scan connected systems for lateral movement evidence\n\n### For Suspicious PowerShell/Command Line Activity:\n1. Capture full command line arguments and script contents\n2. Analyze decoded/deobfuscated content\n3. Determine process execution chain and parent processes\n4. Review executed commands for data access or exfiltration attempts\n5. Check for persistence mechanisms established through scripts\n\n### For Unusual Service Creations:\n1. Document service properties (name, path, startup type, account)\n2. Analyze associated binary files with static/dynamic analysis tools\n3. Review service creation context (user, process, time)\n4. Check for additional modifications to existing services\n5. Monitor service behavior and network communications\n\n## 4. Potential Mitigation Strategies\n\n### For Failed Authentication Attempts:\n* Implement account lockout policies after defined failure thresholds\n* Deploy multi-factor authentication for all remote access\n* Block source IPs demonstrating brute force behavior\n* Implement CAPTCHA for web authentication interfaces\n* Alert on and review all password resets following failed attempts\n\n### For Malware Detections:\n* Isolate infected systems until fully remediated\n* Update antivirus/EDR signatures and perform full system scans\n* Revoke and rotate compromised credentials\n* Block identified command-and-control domains/IPs\n* Restore systems from known clean backups when necessary\n\n### For Suspicious PowerShell/Command Line Activity:\n* Implement PowerShell constrained language mode\n* Enable script block logging and PowerShell transcription\n* Restrict PowerShell execution policies\n* Deploy application whitelisting to prevent unauthorized script execution\n* Remove unnecessary'}
