2025-04-18 10:49:46,810 - security_agent - INFO - Received response from Heroku AI: {'response': '# Privilege Escalation Investigation Analysis\n\n## 1. Security Implications\n\nPrivilege escalation attacks represent a critical security threat where attackers gain elevated permissions beyond their initial access level. The security implications include:\n\n• **Complete system compromise** - Attackers with admin/root privileges can access all system resources\n• **Data exfiltration risk** - Elevated privileges enable access to sensitive information otherwise protected\n• **Persistence establishment** - Attackers can create backdoors and maintain access even after remediation attempts\n• **Lateral movement potential** - Elevated privileges facilitate movement to other systems in the network\n• **Audit trail manipulation** - Administrative access allows attackers to modify or delete logs to hide their activities\n• **Service disruption capability** - Attackers can manipulate critical services, potentially causing system outages\n\n## 2. Specific Indicators to Monitor\n\n### New Service Creation\n• Unexpected service installations outside change management windows\n• Services running with SYSTEM privileges\n• Services with unusual names or descriptions\n• Services with suspicious executable paths or command lines\n• Services created by non-administrative accounts\n\n### Scheduled Task Modification\n• New tasks scheduled by non-administrative users\n• Tasks configured to run with elevated privileges\n• Tasks with obfuscated commands or unusual execution paths\n• Modifications to existing scheduled tasks\n• Tasks scheduled during unusual hours\n\n### Unusual Process Elevation\n• Use of known privilege escalation tools (e.g., PsExec, Mimikatz)\n• Unexpected UAC bypasses or elevation prompts\n• Processes spawning child processes with higher privileges\n• Applications requesting elevated permissions outside normal patterns\n• Use of exploits targeting known vulnerabilities\n\n### Admin Group Changes\n• Unexpected additions to administrative groups\n• Changes to group policy objects affecting permissions\n• Creation of new administrative accounts\n• Modifications to security token privileges\n• Changes to access control lists on sensitive objects\n\n## 3. Investigation Steps\n\n1. **Initial Triage**\n   • Isolate the affected system(s) if active compromise is suspected\n   • Capture volatile data (memory, running processes) before system changes\n   • Establish a timeline of suspicious activities\n\n2. **Log Analysis**\n   • Review security event logs for privilege changes (Event IDs 4672, 4728, 4732, 4738)\n   • Analyze service creation events (Event ID 7045)\n   • Examine scheduled task logs (Microsoft-Windows-TaskScheduler/Operational)\n   • Check PowerShell logs for suspicious commands (Event ID 4104)\n\n3. **System Inspection**\n   • Enumerate all services and compare against baseline\n   • Review scheduled tasks and their execution history\n   • Examine admin group memberships across the environment\n   • Analyze process creation logs for unusual parent-child relationships\n\n4. **Artifact Analysis**\n   • Examine file system for new or modified executables\n   • Check registry for persistence mechanisms\n   • Analyze suspicious binaries in a sandbox environment\n   • Review command history and shell logs\n\n5. **Scope Determination**\n   • Identify all affected systems\n   • Determine initial access vector\n   • Map lateral movement across the environment\n   • Establish complete attacker timeline and activities\n\n## 4. Mitigation Strategies\n\n• **Immediate Containment**\n   - Remove compromised accounts from privileged groups\n   - Disable suspicious services and scheduled tasks\n   - Block associated IPs and domains at network boundaries\n   - Reset compromised credentials\n\n• **Recovery Actions**\n   - Restore systems from known clean backups when possible\n   - Apply all missing security patches\n   - Remove unauthorized services, tasks, and applications\n   - Rebuild heavily compromised systems from scratch\n\n• **Long-term Remediation**\n   - Implement regular privilege reviews and cleanup\n   - Establish robust change management for privileged operations\n   - Deploy application control solutions\n   - Perform regular vulnerability assessments\n\n## 5. Security Controls Implementation\n\n• **Preventative Controls**\n   - Implement the principle of least privilege across all systems\n   - Use Privileged Access Management (PAM) solutions\n   - Deploy application whitelisting to prevent unauthorized executables\n   - Utilize secure baseline configurations for all systems\n   - Enable UAC and configure for maximum security\n\n• **Detective Controls**\n   - Deploy endpoint detection and response (EDR) solutions\n   - Implement Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) with correlation rules\n   - Configure alerts for privilege escalation indicators\n   - Enable comprehensive auditing for privilege use'}
