2025-04-18 10:54:04,154 - security_agent - INFO - Received response from Heroku AI: {'response': '# Privilege Escalation Investigation Analysis\n\n## 1. Security Implications\n\nPrivilege escalation attacks represent a critical security threat where attackers gain elevated access beyond their initial authorization level. The security implications include:\n\n• **Complete system compromise** - Attackers with admin/system privileges can exfiltrate sensitive data, deploy persistent malware, or pivot to other systems\n• **Evasion of security controls** - Elevated privileges allow attackers to disable or bypass security mechanisms\n• **Lateral movement potential** - Higher privileges facilitate movement across network segments and systems\n• **Persistent access** - Administrative access enables creation of backdoors and persistent access mechanisms\n• **Business disruption** - Critical systems may be compromised, leading to operational impacts\n• **Data breach risk** - Unauthorized access to sensitive information with potential regulatory compliance violations\n\n## 2. Specific Indicators to Monitor\n\n### New Service Creation\n• Unexpected service installations outside change management windows\n• Services with unusual names or descriptions\n• Services running with SYSTEM privileges\n• Services with suspicious executable paths or command lines\n• Service binaries with recent creation timestamps\n\n### Scheduled Task Modification\n• New tasks created with elevated privileges\n• Modifications to existing scheduled tasks\n• Tasks that execute scripts or binaries in unusual locations\n• Tasks configured with suspicious command-line parameters\n• Tasks scheduled at unusual times (e.g., middle of the night)\n\n### Unusual Process Elevation\n• Unexpected privilege elevation for standard user processes\n• Use of known privilege escalation tools (e.g., PsExec, mimikatz)\n• Token manipulation activities\n• DLL injection attempts\n• UAC bypass techniques\n• Unexpected child processes of trusted system processes\n\n### Admin Group Changes\n• Additions to privileged groups (Administrators, Domain Admins)\n• Temporary elevation of accounts to admin status\n• Creation of new admin accounts\n• Changes to security group nesting\n• Modifications to access control lists on sensitive objects\n\n## 3. Investigation Steps\n\n1. **Initial Triage**\n   • Identify affected systems and accounts\n   • Preserve volatile evidence (memory, network connections)\n   • Document timeline of suspicious activities\n\n2. **Log Analysis**\n   • Review security event logs (4672, 4648, 4624, 4688 events)\n   • Examine PowerShell logs (4104 events)\n   • Analyze service creation and modification events (7045, 7040)\n   • Check scheduled task history\n\n3. **System Examination**\n   • Analyze running processes and their privilege levels\n   • Review service configurations\n   • Examine scheduled tasks\n   • Investigate startup locations and registry autoruns\n   • Check for unexpected privileged accounts\n\n4. **Behavioral Analysis**\n   • Look for unusual lateral movement\n   • Analyze network connections from privileged processes\n   • Identify abnormal authentication patterns\n   • Monitor sensitive resource access\n\n5. **Artifact Analysis**\n   • Examine file creation timestamps\n   • Check file signatures and publisher information\n   • Analyze script content for malicious commands\n   • Review command history and execution logs\n\n## 4. Mitigation Strategies\n\n• **Immediate Containment**\n  - Isolate affected systems\n  - Remove compromised accounts from privileged groups\n  - Terminate suspicious processes\n  - Disable suspicious services and scheduled tasks\n\n• **Remediation**\n  - Remove malicious software, services, and tasks\n  - Reset compromised credentials\n  - Patch exploited vulnerabilities\n  - Restore systems from clean backups if needed\n  - Reimage heavily compromised systems\n\n• **Recovery Validation**\n  - Verify removal of all malicious components\n  - Confirm system integrity\n  - Test security controls functionality\n  - Monitor for signs of persistence\n\n## 5. Relevant Security Controls\n\n• **Principle of Least Privilege**\n  - Implement role-based access control\n  - Regularly review and audit user permissions\n  - Use just-in-time admin access solutions\n  - Remove standard users from local admin groups\n\n• **Technical Controls**\n  - Enable detailed logging for privilege use (Windows Security Events)\n  - Implement application control/whitelisting\n  - Deploy privileged access management (PAM) solutions\n  - Use Credential Guard and Device Guard\n  - Enable MFA for privileged account access\n\n• **Monitoring and Detection**\n  - Implement continuous monitoring for privilege escalation indicators\n  - Deploy endpoint detection and response (EDR) solutions\n  - Create alerts for suspicious admin group changes\n  - Monitor for new service creation and task scheduling\n  - Establish behavior baselines for privileged accounts\n\n• **Governance**\n  - Document privilege escalation incident response procedures\n  - Conduct regular security awareness training\n  - Perform periodic privilege escalation testing\n  - Maintain up-to-date system inventories and configurations\n  - Implement change management processes for service and task creation'}
